1
00:02:26,713 --> 00:02:29,126
I think it's time
for a little...

2
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:31,794
search-and-destroy mission.

3
00:02:31,818 --> 00:02:33,430
Uh-huh.

4
00:02:54,908 --> 00:02:56,686
Lee. Lee!

5
00:02:56,710 --> 00:02:57,921
Come on, get out!

6
00:02:57,945 --> 00:02:59,225
Let's go. Get out of here.

7
00:03:01,481 --> 00:03:02,681
No, no.

8
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:41,998
Yeah. You know what
I'm talking about. Yeah.

9
00:03:42,022 --> 00:03:43,733
You know, I don't
think those Vietnamese

10
00:03:43,757 --> 00:03:45,768
are gonna be hanging
around here much after tonight.

11
00:03:45,792 --> 00:03:46,769
Let's drink to it!

12
00:03:46,793 --> 00:03:48,338
Yeah. MAN 2: Yes, sir.

13
00:03:48,362 --> 00:03:50,173
Mmm.

14
00:03:50,197 --> 00:03:51,563
You.

15
00:03:52,699 --> 00:03:54,277
Get out of here.

16
00:03:54,301 --> 00:03:55,667
This is an American bar.

17
00:04:01,708 --> 00:04:03,948
My son could have
been killed tonight.

18
00:04:05,012 --> 00:04:07,452
I don't know what you're
talking about, but, uh...

19
00:04:09,282 --> 00:04:12,828
one less gook around here
would be quite an improvement.

20
00:04:12,852 --> 00:04:15,731
Why, you... You
listen to me, Charlie.

21
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:18,168
Now, we don't like your people

22
00:04:18,192 --> 00:04:20,804
fishing our waters
and taking what's ours.

23
00:04:20,828 --> 00:04:22,672
Now, maybe you'll
get the message.

24
00:04:22,696 --> 00:04:26,375
This is an American town
for American fishermen.

25
00:04:26,399 --> 00:04:28,544
So you just pack
up your little brat

26
00:04:28,568 --> 00:04:29,578
and the rest of your people,

27
00:04:29,602 --> 00:04:30,980
and you get the
hell out of here.

28
00:04:31,004 --> 00:04:32,081
Do you hear me?! Huh?!

29
00:04:32,105 --> 00:04:34,273
Put it away, John.

30
00:04:37,611 --> 00:04:38,855
He started it.

31
00:04:38,879 --> 00:04:40,412
You heard me.

32
00:04:41,682 --> 00:04:42,948
Sure.

33
00:04:44,284 --> 00:04:45,724
I don't want no
trouble with the law.

34
00:04:49,289 --> 00:04:50,621
Go home, Mr. Tran.

35
00:04:52,692 --> 00:04:53,736
Why don't you arrest them?

36
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:56,138
You know he burned
my boat tonight.

37
00:04:56,162 --> 00:04:58,508
Knowing and proving it
are two different things.

38
00:04:58,532 --> 00:05:02,144
Does one of my people have
to die before you do something?

39
00:05:02,168 --> 00:05:03,913
This man is behind everything

40
00:05:03,937 --> 00:05:05,214
they have done to us.

41
00:05:05,238 --> 00:05:06,449
You know, he's right, sheriff.

42
00:05:06,473 --> 00:05:08,785
You shut your mouth, old man.

43
00:05:08,809 --> 00:05:10,586
You don't scare me, Sampson.

44
00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:13,478
Tom. Take your friend
and get him out of here.

45
00:05:16,149 --> 00:05:17,309
Let's go, Nguyen.

46
00:05:18,686 --> 00:05:21,831
I'll go. But I can see now.

47
00:05:21,855 --> 00:05:24,335
There's no justice for
Vietnamese in Barton's Bay.

48
00:05:28,461 --> 00:05:30,606
But I'll tell you
this, my friend.

49
00:05:30,630 --> 00:05:34,032
If the law will not
stop them... I will.

50
00:05:36,469 --> 00:05:38,014
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

51
00:05:38,038 --> 00:05:40,598
Nguyen. Nguyen?

52
00:05:45,879 --> 00:05:48,359
I suppose you've been
here all night long, huh?

53
00:05:49,216 --> 00:05:52,062
Yeah. Ask anyone.

54
00:05:52,086 --> 00:05:53,462
That's right. That's right.

55
00:05:53,486 --> 00:05:54,686
Yeah. He was here.

56
00:05:56,023 --> 00:05:58,263
One of these days,
I'm gonna put you away.

57
00:06:00,027 --> 00:06:02,304
Well, you better hurry, sheriff.

58
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:06,443
Election's less than
eight weeks away.

59
00:06:07,834 --> 00:06:08,834
Ah.

60
00:06:24,117 --> 00:06:26,262
Oh, man, you gotta be kidding.

61
00:06:26,286 --> 00:06:29,365
Get off my boat
before I throw you off.

62
00:07:48,035 --> 00:07:51,635
Gangway. It's the prosecutor.

63
00:07:53,673 --> 00:07:56,040
You wanna tell me why
you've got Nguyen Tran in jail?

64
00:07:58,245 --> 00:07:59,789
You heard what
happened last night.

65
00:07:59,813 --> 00:08:02,625
Nguyen Tran threatened
John Sampson.

66
00:08:02,649 --> 00:08:03,927
Is that all you've got?

67
00:08:03,951 --> 00:08:06,896
I gotta hold him for
questioning for 48 hours.

68
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:08,560
Look, I wanna
show you something.

69
00:08:09,823 --> 00:08:11,600
The way I figure it...

70
00:08:11,624 --> 00:08:14,938
John was stabbed
inside the cabin.

71
00:08:14,962 --> 00:08:16,806
Notice the trail of
blood leading from inside

72
00:08:16,830 --> 00:08:18,750
to where the body was
discovered this morning.

73
00:08:20,334 --> 00:08:22,111
Where's the murder weapon?

74
00:08:22,135 --> 00:08:23,379
It's not in the boat.

75
00:08:23,403 --> 00:08:25,283
Sheriff, he's coming up.

76
00:08:29,809 --> 00:08:32,510
Lying on the bottom just
as plain as day, sheriff.

77
00:08:37,751 --> 00:08:40,196
See the initials
carved into the handle?

78
00:08:40,220 --> 00:08:42,165
"N.T."

79
00:08:42,189 --> 00:08:44,823
I'd say this knife
belongs to Nguyen Tran.

80
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:49,705
I've done my job,
Mr. Prosecutor.

81
00:08:49,729 --> 00:08:51,829
Now you gotta do yours.

82
00:08:53,767 --> 00:08:54,944
Hey, Michelle?

83
00:08:54,968 --> 00:08:56,178
I'm gonna buy a new car.

84
00:08:56,202 --> 00:08:58,147
Got regular radio dials.

85
00:08:58,171 --> 00:08:59,915
It's worth buying just for that.

86
00:09:02,976 --> 00:09:04,720
Mr. Matlock? Yes, sir?

87
00:09:04,744 --> 00:09:08,691
I'm Tom Jakes, the
harbormaster out at Barton's Bay.

88
00:09:08,715 --> 00:09:09,692
Oh.

89
00:09:09,716 --> 00:09:11,160
You heard about
the trouble there?

90
00:09:11,184 --> 00:09:12,695
A little.

91
00:09:12,719 --> 00:09:14,230
This is Lee Tran.

92
00:09:14,254 --> 00:09:16,599
His father's been
charged with the murder.

93
00:09:16,623 --> 00:09:18,968
My father didn't do it.

94
00:09:18,992 --> 00:09:21,392
We came here to
hire you to defend him.

95
00:09:23,397 --> 00:09:24,474
Oh, well.

96
00:09:24,498 --> 00:09:26,375
Sir, I know you're expensive.

97
00:09:26,399 --> 00:09:28,711
But I have some holdings,

98
00:09:28,735 --> 00:09:31,313
some stocks, some bonds.

99
00:09:31,337 --> 00:09:34,784
And I could mortgage my
boat if need be, for the rest.

100
00:09:34,808 --> 00:09:37,653
Mr. Tran must be
a very good friend.

101
00:09:37,677 --> 00:09:40,222
I brought him and
Lee to this country.

102
00:09:40,246 --> 00:09:41,524
I was their sponsor.

103
00:09:41,548 --> 00:09:43,526
Oh.

104
00:09:43,550 --> 00:09:45,894
My father's a good man.

105
00:09:45,918 --> 00:09:47,352
He wouldn't kill anyone.

106
00:09:55,595 --> 00:09:56,839
Oh, you're Ben Matlock?

107
00:09:56,863 --> 00:09:57,840
Yes, sir.

108
00:09:57,864 --> 00:09:59,241
Michael Barton, the prosecutor.

109
00:09:59,265 --> 00:10:02,344
Oh. This is my associate,
Michelle Thomas.

110
00:10:02,368 --> 00:10:03,979
Hello. Hi.

111
00:10:04,003 --> 00:10:05,381
Uh, Barton?

112
00:10:05,405 --> 00:10:07,950
I don't think I ever met
anybody alive before

113
00:10:07,974 --> 00:10:09,285
who had a town named after him.

114
00:10:09,309 --> 00:10:10,753
Oh. Not me.

115
00:10:10,777 --> 00:10:13,355
Uh, Barton's Bay was named
after my great-grandfather.

116
00:10:13,379 --> 00:10:14,790
Oh.

117
00:10:14,814 --> 00:10:16,925
Well, I guess you're
looking for the sheriff's office.

118
00:10:16,949 --> 00:10:18,261
It's right in there.

119
00:10:18,285 --> 00:10:19,462
Oh. Great.

120
00:10:19,486 --> 00:10:20,997
Well, I'll see you
later. I'll see you.

121
00:10:21,021 --> 00:10:22,021
Nice to meet you.

122
00:10:23,323 --> 00:10:25,267
I'm glad you could take
the case, Mr. Matlock.

123
00:10:25,291 --> 00:10:26,602
Yeah?

124
00:10:26,626 --> 00:10:28,337
Tom Jakes is an old
friend of the family.

125
00:10:28,361 --> 00:10:30,472
After Nguyen was charged,
he asked me if I could

126
00:10:30,496 --> 00:10:32,141
recommend an attorney for him.

127
00:10:32,165 --> 00:10:33,175
You recommended me?

128
00:10:33,199 --> 00:10:34,343
Yes, I did.

129
00:10:34,367 --> 00:10:36,946
There's a very strong
case against Nguyen,

130
00:10:36,970 --> 00:10:38,347
and I wanted to
make sure he could get

131
00:10:38,371 --> 00:10:39,949
as good a representation
as possible.

132
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:43,485
Oh, well... thank you.

133
00:10:43,509 --> 00:10:46,429
Yeah. Uh, jail's
down there. Okay.

134
00:10:52,885 --> 00:10:54,930
Uh, tell me this.

135
00:10:54,954 --> 00:10:56,198
Did you make a threat

136
00:10:56,222 --> 00:10:59,868
against John Sampson that night?

137
00:10:59,892 --> 00:11:01,303
Yes.

138
00:11:01,327 --> 00:11:04,707
But when I went home...

139
00:11:04,731 --> 00:11:07,091
I knew I couldn't
commit violence.

140
00:11:10,537 --> 00:11:12,682
Your knife was
found in the water

141
00:11:12,706 --> 00:11:14,517
directly under his boat.

142
00:11:14,541 --> 00:11:17,219
I did not kill this man.

143
00:11:17,243 --> 00:11:19,989
My knife was stolen when
they robbed my boat last week.

144
00:11:20,013 --> 00:11:21,857
It wasn't on the theft report.

145
00:11:21,881 --> 00:11:24,427
When you consider
everything they stole from me:

146
00:11:24,451 --> 00:11:28,498
my... my radio, my nets,

147
00:11:28,522 --> 00:11:30,299
all my fishing equipments,

148
00:11:30,323 --> 00:11:33,169
the knife is insignificant.

149
00:11:33,193 --> 00:11:34,892
So you didn't report it?

150
00:11:39,532 --> 00:11:41,811
Mr. Matlock...

151
00:11:41,835 --> 00:11:44,279
I would do anything
to protect my son.

152
00:11:44,303 --> 00:11:46,515
Yes, even kill.

153
00:11:46,539 --> 00:11:49,741
But I did not kill John Sampson.

154
00:12:00,220 --> 00:12:04,300
Well... I guess the first thing

155
00:12:04,324 --> 00:12:06,790
is see if we can
get you a fair trial.

156
00:12:10,230 --> 00:12:13,442
I don't see grounds
for a change of venue.

157
00:12:13,466 --> 00:12:15,945
Those people out
there are grounds.

158
00:12:15,969 --> 00:12:18,413
With all due respect,
Your Honor, I don't...

159
00:12:18,437 --> 00:12:21,784
I don't think my client
can get a fair trial here.

160
00:12:21,808 --> 00:12:24,386
Mr. Matlock, please don't
condemn an entire community

161
00:12:24,410 --> 00:12:26,221
because of a few
people in the street.

162
00:12:26,245 --> 00:12:27,945
Well... Uh, Mr. Barton.

163
00:12:29,516 --> 00:12:31,060
This is a decent community...

164
00:12:31,084 --> 00:12:34,363
with scores of hardworking,
fair-minded people.

165
00:12:34,387 --> 00:12:36,132
I'm sure that's true.

166
00:12:36,156 --> 00:12:39,368
They just don't seem to be
very fond of the Vietnamese.

167
00:12:39,392 --> 00:12:42,338
Mr. Matlock, most of our people

168
00:12:42,362 --> 00:12:44,602
have great sympathy
for the Vietnamese.

169
00:12:45,832 --> 00:12:47,409
Your Honor, you know
I served in Vietnam.

170
00:12:47,433 --> 00:12:49,045
And I'm aware of the problems

171
00:12:49,069 --> 00:12:50,213
they've been having here.

172
00:12:50,237 --> 00:12:52,414
But I have great
respect and admiration

173
00:12:52,438 --> 00:12:53,816
for the people of
this community.

174
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:56,540
I believe Mr. Tran
<i>can</i> get a fair trial.

175
00:12:57,877 --> 00:13:00,456
I'm afraid I have to
agree with Mr. Barton.

176
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:01,857
The trial will take place here.

177
00:13:01,881 --> 00:13:03,526
Your motion for

178
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:05,282
change of venue,
Mr. Matlock, is denied.

179
00:13:15,428 --> 00:13:17,173
You okay, Dad?

180
00:13:17,197 --> 00:13:19,175
I'm fine, son.

181
00:13:19,199 --> 00:13:21,210
Uh, Mr. Matlock,

182
00:13:21,234 --> 00:13:23,078
who put up the
money for my bail?

183
00:13:23,102 --> 00:13:26,015
Uh, M-Mr. Barton...
The judge released you

184
00:13:26,039 --> 00:13:28,284
on your own recognizance.

185
00:13:28,308 --> 00:13:30,119
Thank you, Mr. Barton.

186
00:13:30,143 --> 00:13:32,488
You have always been kind to us.

187
00:13:32,512 --> 00:13:34,056
Uh, we're done.
Better get going.

188
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:35,346
Yeah.

189
00:13:46,993 --> 00:13:48,537
Ah.

190
00:13:48,561 --> 00:13:51,507
Ah, excuse me. Excuse
me, that's a new car.

191
00:13:51,531 --> 00:13:52,975
That's a new car.

192
00:13:52,999 --> 00:13:55,010
Sooner or later it will
get a few scratches.

193
00:13:55,034 --> 00:13:56,467
Now, listen.

194
00:14:02,074 --> 00:14:03,152
Break it up.

195
00:14:03,176 --> 00:14:04,486
Whose side are you on?

196
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:06,277
I said, break it up.

197
00:14:10,483 --> 00:14:11,494
You too.

198
00:14:15,655 --> 00:14:19,034
Thank you, uh...
Thank you, Deputy, uh...

199
00:14:19,058 --> 00:14:21,003
Atkins. Thank you.

200
00:14:21,027 --> 00:14:24,762
Sooner or later...
Hey, Michelle, uh...

201
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:29,445
get Mr. Tran and Lee

202
00:14:29,469 --> 00:14:31,881
home safely and meet
me back at the motel.

203
00:14:31,905 --> 00:14:33,049
Where are you going?

204
00:14:33,073 --> 00:14:36,552
That's the guy
that found the knife.

205
00:14:36,576 --> 00:14:37,753
You know, in the water.

206
00:14:37,777 --> 00:14:39,354
Yeah... Are you gonna be safe?

207
00:14:39,378 --> 00:14:40,912
I'm going to the
safest place in town.

208
00:14:59,199 --> 00:15:02,711
I went through John
Sampson's house and his boat.

209
00:15:02,735 --> 00:15:04,380
Didn't find a single item

210
00:15:04,404 --> 00:15:06,715
belonging to any of
those Vietnamese.

211
00:15:06,739 --> 00:15:09,318
Oh, uh... I know.

212
00:15:09,342 --> 00:15:12,654
Mr. Tran claims
his knife was stolen.

213
00:15:12,678 --> 00:15:15,213
I guess in his place
that's what I would say.

214
00:15:16,349 --> 00:15:17,793
Of course, if he's
telling the truth,

215
00:15:17,817 --> 00:15:20,351
then whoever stole it
killed John Sampson.

216
00:15:21,554 --> 00:15:22,931
Maybe.

217
00:15:22,955 --> 00:15:25,434
Find anybody who had a motive...

218
00:15:25,458 --> 00:15:27,136
and I'll be all ears.

219
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:29,059
How'd you feel
about Mr. Sampson?

220
00:15:30,296 --> 00:15:31,576
Motive, huh?

221
00:15:33,266 --> 00:15:35,211
Well, let me put
it to you this way:

222
00:15:35,235 --> 00:15:38,769
As far as I'm concerned,
Tran did this town a favor.

223
00:15:39,873 --> 00:15:41,105
Hm.

224
00:15:42,175 --> 00:15:44,020
I hear that, uh...

225
00:15:44,044 --> 00:15:46,355
John Sampson was
thinking about running

226
00:15:46,379 --> 00:15:48,891
against you for sheriff,
and the way the feelings

227
00:15:48,915 --> 00:15:51,682
are going about the Vietnamese,
he had a good chance of winning.

228
00:15:53,485 --> 00:15:54,997
So you think I killed him?

229
00:15:55,021 --> 00:15:57,299
No. Just telling you something

230
00:15:57,323 --> 00:15:59,201
I learned about
motives a long time ago.

231
00:15:59,225 --> 00:16:01,125
You never can tell
who might have one.

232
00:16:47,973 --> 00:16:49,373
What are you doing here?

233
00:16:51,343 --> 00:16:54,256
Oh. Uh, Mrs. Sampson, uh...

234
00:16:54,280 --> 00:16:57,426
picture in the paper
doesn't do you justice.

235
00:16:57,450 --> 00:16:58,660
I asked you a question.

236
00:16:58,684 --> 00:17:00,329
I-I'm Ben Matlock.

237
00:17:03,256 --> 00:17:04,767
Please get off my boat.

238
00:17:04,791 --> 00:17:05,968
I need to talk to you.

239
00:17:05,992 --> 00:17:08,072
You're defending
my husband's killer.

240
00:17:13,566 --> 00:17:16,211
I just came by to pick up
my husband's possessions,

241
00:17:16,235 --> 00:17:18,214
so can you please make it brief?

242
00:17:18,238 --> 00:17:20,938
Well, I... I just have
one question, really.

243
00:17:22,108 --> 00:17:24,286
Why was your husband
living on his boat?

244
00:17:24,310 --> 00:17:26,454
I... I don't know a
lot about fishermen,

245
00:17:26,478 --> 00:17:29,091
but... I don't know many

246
00:17:29,115 --> 00:17:31,915
that keep their good
clothes on their boat.

247
00:17:37,957 --> 00:17:39,490
We've been having
a lot of problems.

248
00:17:41,060 --> 00:17:42,304
Marital.

249
00:17:42,328 --> 00:17:44,373
Well, was, uh...

250
00:17:44,397 --> 00:17:46,757
Was he seeing another woman?

251
00:17:47,834 --> 00:17:48,834
No.

252
00:17:50,036 --> 00:17:53,771
Um... you seeing another man?

253
00:17:54,741 --> 00:17:56,039
No, I was not.

254
00:17:57,510 --> 00:17:59,410
Uh... I, uh...

255
00:18:01,948 --> 00:18:07,285
Uh, I heard he... has
a pretty mean temper.

256
00:18:10,489 --> 00:18:11,800
You're out of line.

257
00:18:11,824 --> 00:18:13,468
Oh, yes. I-I am.

258
00:18:13,492 --> 00:18:14,603
I apologize.

259
00:18:14,627 --> 00:18:18,073
I'm... I'm sorry. I...
uh... I'll be going.

260
00:18:18,097 --> 00:18:20,657
And maybe... May...
Talk another time.

261
00:19:28,934 --> 00:19:30,300
Come on, Ben.

262
00:19:40,980 --> 00:19:43,780
Stop a minute and
let's get our breath.

263
00:19:43,883 --> 00:19:45,460
Phew.

264
00:19:45,484 --> 00:19:47,095
How'd you know I was here?

265
00:19:47,119 --> 00:19:49,364
When you didn't
show up at the motel,

266
00:19:49,388 --> 00:19:50,699
I got a little worried.

267
00:19:50,723 --> 00:19:52,801
A little worry's
good for a person.

268
00:19:52,825 --> 00:19:53,924
I appreciate it.

269
00:19:55,261 --> 00:19:57,239
How about dropping
me by Mr. Tran's?

270
00:19:57,263 --> 00:20:00,676
Uh, wait, wait. Then
drive over to, uh...

271
00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:02,844
To this place.

272
00:20:02,868 --> 00:20:04,279
It's, uh, in Greenville.

273
00:20:04,303 --> 00:20:06,781
About 60 miles, just
across the county line.

274
00:20:06,805 --> 00:20:07,816
"Starlight Motel."

275
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:09,206
Yeah. Now, here:

276
00:20:10,509 --> 00:20:13,689
This is a picture
of Ms. Sampson.

277
00:20:13,713 --> 00:20:15,345
Show it around.

278
00:20:37,102 --> 00:20:38,079
Welcome to our home.

279
00:20:38,103 --> 00:20:40,249
Ha. Thanks. Hey.

280
00:20:40,273 --> 00:20:41,550
Ah-ha-ha.

281
00:20:41,574 --> 00:20:43,018
Hello, Mr. Matlock.

282
00:20:43,042 --> 00:20:45,020
Why, hello, Lee.

283
00:20:45,044 --> 00:20:48,557
Mm-mm. Something smells good.

284
00:20:48,581 --> 00:20:51,193
We're having all
specialties in your honor.

285
00:20:51,217 --> 00:20:54,062
First, we'll start with
<i>canh chua dau ca,</i>

286
00:20:54,086 --> 00:20:57,655
then<i> goi sua tom thit,</i> and
then some<i> ca chien nuoc mam.</i>

287
00:20:59,758 --> 00:21:03,438
Dried jellyfish with
cabbage, sour fish head soup,

288
00:21:03,462 --> 00:21:05,462
and fried fish in fish sauce.

289
00:21:10,036 --> 00:21:12,413
I'd like some more
of this fried jellyfish.

290
00:21:12,437 --> 00:21:15,283
Oh. Please take
as much as you like.

291
00:21:15,307 --> 00:21:16,818
That should be about enough.

292
00:21:16,842 --> 00:21:17,842
I'm stuffed.

293
00:21:25,217 --> 00:21:26,495
Excuse me, Mr. Matlock.

294
00:21:26,519 --> 00:21:27,896
I have to wash the dishes.

295
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:28,953
Oh. Yeah.

296
00:21:33,092 --> 00:21:34,191
Mm.

297
00:21:38,630 --> 00:21:39,608
Nice boy.

298
00:21:39,632 --> 00:21:42,711
Yes.

299
00:21:42,735 --> 00:21:43,945
That's why I'm afraid.

300
00:21:43,969 --> 00:21:46,581
When Tom Jakes first
brought us over here,

301
00:21:46,605 --> 00:21:49,172
I had such high hopes.

302
00:21:50,510 --> 00:21:52,654
Now...

303
00:21:52,678 --> 00:21:55,590
I fear what would happen
to him if I have to go to jail.

304
00:21:55,614 --> 00:21:58,927
Oh, you're a long ways
from that, Mr. Tran.

305
00:21:58,951 --> 00:22:00,895
Maybe these people are right.

306
00:22:00,919 --> 00:22:01,963
Maybe the Vietnamese

307
00:22:01,987 --> 00:22:04,065
have no right to be here.

308
00:22:04,089 --> 00:22:05,556
But my son does.

309
00:22:09,094 --> 00:22:12,229
You see... he's half American.

310
00:22:14,767 --> 00:22:15,847
He's not your son?

311
00:22:17,170 --> 00:22:18,170
My stepson.

312
00:22:19,571 --> 00:22:21,516
He is what is
called "Amerasian."

313
00:22:21,540 --> 00:22:23,518
His real father was
an American soldier

314
00:22:23,542 --> 00:22:26,102
who was killed during the war.

315
00:22:28,147 --> 00:22:30,480
I married his mother
before he was born.

316
00:22:33,485 --> 00:22:34,918
She was so beautiful.

317
00:22:36,689 --> 00:22:38,133
She was beautiful.

318
00:22:38,157 --> 00:22:39,634
What happened?

319
00:22:39,658 --> 00:22:43,293
Our village was bombed,
and she was killed.

320
00:22:45,064 --> 00:22:46,341
He was only 2 then.

321
00:22:46,365 --> 00:22:48,510
But before she died,

322
00:22:48,534 --> 00:22:51,179
I promise her I will
always take care of Lee.

323
00:22:51,203 --> 00:22:53,771
Hm.

324
00:22:56,809 --> 00:22:58,009
Here's your Miss Thomas.

325
00:22:58,944 --> 00:23:00,188
Oh.

326
00:23:00,212 --> 00:23:01,690
Hello, Mr. Tran. Hi.

327
00:23:01,714 --> 00:23:02,925
Hey, Michelle, come here.

328
00:23:02,949 --> 00:23:04,626
Come here a minute. Here, uh...

329
00:23:04,650 --> 00:23:06,095
Here... Yeah, uh, good.

330
00:23:06,119 --> 00:23:07,095
Try some of this.

331
00:23:07,119 --> 00:23:08,896
Try some of this. This is...

332
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:12,033
This is, uh... This
is, uh, dried jellyfish

333
00:23:12,057 --> 00:23:14,257
and sour fish head soup.

334
00:23:15,627 --> 00:23:17,039
How'd you do?

335
00:23:17,063 --> 00:23:19,407
Um... Mrs. Sampson

336
00:23:19,431 --> 00:23:21,309
was a regular customer
at the Starlight Motel.

337
00:23:21,333 --> 00:23:23,611
Yeah? Sometimes twice a week.

338
00:23:23,635 --> 00:23:24,613
Who with?

339
00:23:24,637 --> 00:23:26,404
He's right here.

340
00:23:28,007 --> 00:23:29,007
The sheriff?

341
00:23:31,210 --> 00:23:33,288
Deputy Atkins? Yeah.

342
00:23:33,312 --> 00:23:34,312
Mr. Tran?

343
00:23:36,015 --> 00:23:38,527
What do you know
about Deputy Atkins?

344
00:23:38,551 --> 00:23:41,530
Well, he was a fisherman
until about six months ago.

345
00:23:41,554 --> 00:23:44,132
He lost his boat to the bank.

346
00:23:44,156 --> 00:23:46,268
Oh. Deputy Atkins.

347
00:24:07,813 --> 00:24:10,792
Under the circumstances...

348
00:24:10,816 --> 00:24:13,362
I see no alternative but
to reconsider Mr. Matlock's

349
00:24:13,386 --> 00:24:16,464
request for a change of venue.

350
00:24:16,488 --> 00:24:18,566
Motion granted.

351
00:24:18,590 --> 00:24:20,490
The trial will be held
in Fulton County.

352
00:24:36,308 --> 00:24:39,721
After finding Mr. Sampson's
body on the deck,

353
00:24:39,745 --> 00:24:41,623
what did you do then?

354
00:24:41,647 --> 00:24:42,924
I secured the crime scene

355
00:24:42,948 --> 00:24:44,993
and commenced my investigation.

356
00:24:45,017 --> 00:24:46,928
Did that include looking
for a murder weapon?

357
00:24:46,952 --> 00:24:48,163
Yes, sir.

358
00:24:48,187 --> 00:24:50,499
Deputy Atkins is
a qualified diver.

359
00:24:50,523 --> 00:24:53,201
He began an underwater search
of the area beneath the boat.

360
00:24:53,225 --> 00:24:55,003
He came up with the knife

361
00:24:55,027 --> 00:24:57,427
within a few
minutes of his dive.

362
00:25:02,134 --> 00:25:05,314
I show you People's Exhibit 5...

363
00:25:05,338 --> 00:25:07,378
and I ask you, do you
recognize it, sheriff?

364
00:25:10,075 --> 00:25:11,542
Yes, sir. That's the knife.

365
00:25:12,678 --> 00:25:14,623
Yesterday, the coroner testified

366
00:25:14,647 --> 00:25:16,324
that the victim's wound

367
00:25:16,348 --> 00:25:20,762
was consistent with
the blade on this knife.

368
00:25:20,786 --> 00:25:22,764
Sheriff, can you tell us

369
00:25:22,788 --> 00:25:25,321
if there are any identifying
marks on this knife?

370
00:25:26,591 --> 00:25:28,336
Yes, sir. Right
there on the handle.

371
00:25:28,360 --> 00:25:30,071
You can see the initials N.T.

372
00:25:30,095 --> 00:25:31,373
It's a common custom

373
00:25:31,397 --> 00:25:34,009
in the Barton's Bay area
for the local fishermen

374
00:25:34,033 --> 00:25:36,678
to personalize their
fishing knives, is that so?

375
00:25:36,702 --> 00:25:39,013
Uh, Your Honor...

376
00:25:39,037 --> 00:25:40,949
I'll object, for the record,

377
00:25:40,973 --> 00:25:43,952
that the question is leading
and without foundation, however,

378
00:25:43,976 --> 00:25:46,621
I know what
counsel is attempting,

379
00:25:46,645 --> 00:25:48,823
so the defense will
stipulate that Exhibit 5

380
00:25:48,847 --> 00:25:51,159
is Mr. Tran's knife.

381
00:25:51,183 --> 00:25:52,427
Thank you, Mr. Matlock.

382
00:25:52,451 --> 00:25:54,195
Nothing further.

383
00:25:54,219 --> 00:25:55,864
Cross-examine.

384
00:25:55,888 --> 00:25:58,288
Thank you. Uh...

385
00:25:59,024 --> 00:26:02,237
No one is disputing...

386
00:26:02,261 --> 00:26:04,939
that this is Mr. Tran's knife.

387
00:26:04,963 --> 00:26:07,476
But sheriff, you didn't see

388
00:26:07,500 --> 00:26:12,046
Mr. Tran stab
Mr. Sampson, did you?

389
00:26:12,070 --> 00:26:13,248
No.

390
00:26:13,272 --> 00:26:15,950
And in the course
of your investigation,

391
00:26:15,974 --> 00:26:19,688
you didn't find one single
person who had seen Mr. Tran

392
00:26:19,712 --> 00:26:23,592
use this or any other
knife on Mr. Sampson.

393
00:26:23,616 --> 00:26:24,593
Isn't that right?

394
00:26:24,617 --> 00:26:26,361
Yeah, that's right.

395
00:26:26,385 --> 00:26:29,063
However, in the weeks
before the murder,

396
00:26:29,087 --> 00:26:31,800
there were a string of
robberies on Vietnamese boats,

397
00:26:31,824 --> 00:26:32,834
weren't there?

398
00:26:32,858 --> 00:26:33,935
We had some reports.

399
00:26:33,959 --> 00:26:36,438
And, uh, a few days
before the murder,

400
00:26:36,462 --> 00:26:38,273
Mr. Tran's own boat...

401
00:26:38,297 --> 00:26:39,841
was robbed, wasn't it?

402
00:26:39,865 --> 00:26:42,110
I had a report.

403
00:26:42,134 --> 00:26:43,511
Saw evidence of a break-in.

404
00:26:43,535 --> 00:26:45,280
Mr. Tran didn't specifically

405
00:26:45,304 --> 00:26:47,716
list his knife as
stolen, did he?

406
00:26:47,740 --> 00:26:48,883
He gave me a list of things.

407
00:26:48,907 --> 00:26:50,418
That wasn't on it. Uh-huh.

408
00:26:50,442 --> 00:26:54,722
However, on the list
was the bridge compass,

409
00:26:54,746 --> 00:26:56,791
radio, and all fishing nets.

410
00:26:56,815 --> 00:27:00,128
That's all pretty
large, expensive items.

411
00:27:00,152 --> 00:27:02,930
It's not unusual for a citizen
to overlook smaller items

412
00:27:02,954 --> 00:27:05,199
when their larger,
more expensive ones

413
00:27:05,223 --> 00:27:06,568
have been stolen, isn't it?

414
00:27:06,592 --> 00:27:08,403
No. No.

415
00:27:08,427 --> 00:27:10,338
Now... Now the
coroner says the time

416
00:27:10,362 --> 00:27:13,408
of Mr. Sampson's
death was, uh, 1 a.m.

417
00:27:13,432 --> 00:27:15,610
It's pretty quiet out on
the harbor then, isn't it?

418
00:27:15,634 --> 00:27:16,611
Yes.

419
00:27:16,635 --> 00:27:18,035
I mean, it's real quiet.

420
00:27:19,671 --> 00:27:21,983
Uh, my associate,
Miss Thomas and I,

421
00:27:22,007 --> 00:27:23,985
went out to Mr. Sampson's boat

422
00:27:24,009 --> 00:27:27,622
at 1 a.m., and I
went in... In the cabin,

423
00:27:27,646 --> 00:27:30,725
where Mr. Sampson was
standing when he was stabbed.

424
00:27:30,749 --> 00:27:35,596
And my associate
tried to sneak up on me.

425
00:27:35,620 --> 00:27:38,633
You know, the way the
killer must have done.

426
00:27:38,657 --> 00:27:42,336
And no matter how
careful she was, I heard her.

427
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:45,339
She even took off her
shoes, and I still heard her.

428
00:27:45,363 --> 00:27:46,341
Objection.

429
00:27:46,365 --> 00:27:47,608
Relevancy.

430
00:27:47,632 --> 00:27:49,010
It isn't even a question.

431
00:27:49,034 --> 00:27:51,680
She took off her shoes,
and I still heard her.

432
00:27:51,704 --> 00:27:52,681
How about that?

433
00:27:52,705 --> 00:27:53,782
Objection.

434
00:27:53,806 --> 00:27:54,849
Sustained.

435
00:27:54,873 --> 00:27:56,372
I apologize to both of you.

436
00:28:01,247 --> 00:28:04,226
Now... as... As we all know,

437
00:28:04,250 --> 00:28:06,494
Mr. Sampson was
stabbed in the back.

438
00:28:06,518 --> 00:28:09,965
Doesn't that lead to the
obvious conclusion that either

439
00:28:09,989 --> 00:28:12,234
whoever did it
sneaked up on him,

440
00:28:12,258 --> 00:28:15,470
or Mr. Sampson knew
his killer well enough

441
00:28:15,494 --> 00:28:16,671
to turn his back on him?

442
00:28:16,695 --> 00:28:17,672
I suppose.

443
00:28:17,696 --> 00:28:18,840
Now, let me put it another way.

444
00:28:18,864 --> 00:28:20,475
Given what had transpired

445
00:28:20,499 --> 00:28:22,678
between Mr. Tran
and Mr. Sampson,

446
00:28:22,702 --> 00:28:24,646
you certainly wouldn't
expect Mr. Sampson

447
00:28:24,670 --> 00:28:27,015
to turn his back on Mr. Tran.

448
00:28:27,039 --> 00:28:30,184
So that has to mean that
Mr. Sampson knew or...

449
00:28:30,208 --> 00:28:31,219
Objection. Objection.

450
00:28:31,243 --> 00:28:32,821
Calls for speculation,
no relevancy...

451
00:28:32,845 --> 00:28:34,155
Withdrew the question.

452
00:28:34,179 --> 00:28:35,423
No further questions.

453
00:28:35,447 --> 00:28:36,613
Withdraw the question.

454
00:28:39,218 --> 00:28:40,895
Isn't it true that you
and the defendant

455
00:28:40,919 --> 00:28:41,963
are close friends?

456
00:28:41,987 --> 00:28:43,231
Yes, we are.

457
00:28:43,255 --> 00:28:47,235
Isn't it also true that
you sponsored Mr. Tran

458
00:28:47,259 --> 00:28:49,270
and his stepson when they
first came to Barton's Bay?

459
00:28:49,294 --> 00:28:51,695
Everybody knows that, Mickey.

460
00:28:54,366 --> 00:28:56,833
Please, Mr. Jakes. Just
answer the question.

461
00:28:57,836 --> 00:29:00,281
Yes, I sponsored them.

462
00:29:00,305 --> 00:29:02,650
And on the night of the murder,

463
00:29:02,674 --> 00:29:06,121
weren't you in the Lucky
Catch bar when Mr. Tran

464
00:29:06,145 --> 00:29:08,890
threatened Mr. Sampson?

465
00:29:08,914 --> 00:29:10,291
Well, yeah.

466
00:29:10,315 --> 00:29:11,981
What did he say?

467
00:29:13,018 --> 00:29:13,995
Your Honor,

468
00:29:14,019 --> 00:29:15,363
instruct this
witness to answer...

469
00:29:15,387 --> 00:29:16,965
Well, you don't
need to say that.

470
00:29:16,989 --> 00:29:19,556
What he said was...

471
00:29:21,360 --> 00:29:25,673
that if the sheriff didn't
stop John Sampson...

472
00:29:25,697 --> 00:29:26,930
that he would.

473
00:29:28,167 --> 00:29:30,207
Thank you. Nothing further.

474
00:29:35,140 --> 00:29:37,318
Sorry about that, Nguyen.

475
00:29:37,342 --> 00:29:39,262
You only told the truth, Tom.

476
00:29:40,212 --> 00:29:42,023
Why are you doing
this to my father?

477
00:29:42,047 --> 00:29:43,225
I thought you were our friend?

478
00:29:43,249 --> 00:29:44,392
I am. I...

479
00:29:44,416 --> 00:29:46,827
Lee. Mr. Barton's
only doing his job.

480
00:29:46,851 --> 00:29:48,596
Just like the rest of
them. You hate us.

481
00:29:48,620 --> 00:29:49,620
Lee.

482
00:29:52,791 --> 00:29:54,135
I'm sorry, Mr. Barton.

483
00:29:54,159 --> 00:29:56,704
He, uh... He doesn't understand.

484
00:29:56,728 --> 00:29:58,728
I'll talk to him.

485
00:30:01,033 --> 00:30:03,378
It's all so senseless.

486
00:30:03,402 --> 00:30:04,612
When the Vietnamese first came

487
00:30:04,636 --> 00:30:06,181
to Barton's Bay three years ago,

488
00:30:06,205 --> 00:30:07,415
there were no problems.

489
00:30:07,439 --> 00:30:09,184
Everybody got along.

490
00:30:09,208 --> 00:30:10,518
Till about six months ago.

491
00:30:10,542 --> 00:30:11,987
When the fishing got bad,

492
00:30:12,011 --> 00:30:13,388
that's when it all started.

493
00:30:13,412 --> 00:30:16,291
The local fishermen got
scared. And like scared people,

494
00:30:16,315 --> 00:30:19,227
they looked for a convenient
scapegoat: the Vietnamese.

495
00:30:19,251 --> 00:30:20,595
It's such a shame...

496
00:30:20,619 --> 00:30:22,630
when you realize everybody knows

497
00:30:22,654 --> 00:30:24,032
what caused the problem.

498
00:30:24,056 --> 00:30:25,533
What's that?

499
00:30:25,557 --> 00:30:28,536
We had an
unseasonably cool winter.

500
00:30:28,560 --> 00:30:32,240
The cold Arctic currents
cooled off the local waters.

501
00:30:32,264 --> 00:30:35,076
The cool waters
caused abnormal tides

502
00:30:35,100 --> 00:30:36,444
and shifting currents,

503
00:30:36,468 --> 00:30:38,813
and chased away the red
snapper and the mackerel.

504
00:30:38,837 --> 00:30:40,148
Huh.

505
00:30:40,172 --> 00:30:41,449
You say the fishermen know that?

506
00:30:41,473 --> 00:30:43,984
Of course. Ask Tom
Jakes. He'll tell you.

507
00:30:44,008 --> 00:30:47,422
Well, uh... I'll
see you tomorrow.

508
00:30:47,446 --> 00:30:49,090
Okay.

509
00:30:49,114 --> 00:30:50,625
Heh.

510
00:30:50,649 --> 00:30:52,148
Give me one all the way.

511
00:30:53,752 --> 00:30:55,229
Hi. Hi.

512
00:30:55,253 --> 00:30:56,230
Want one?

513
00:30:56,254 --> 00:30:58,199
No.

514
00:30:58,223 --> 00:30:59,534
I found out a few things.

515
00:30:59,558 --> 00:31:01,002
Yep?

516
00:31:01,026 --> 00:31:02,771
Deputy Atkins is still having

517
00:31:02,795 --> 00:31:05,128
his trysts with Mrs. Sampson.

518
00:31:06,532 --> 00:31:08,410
He was an MP in Vietnam.

519
00:31:08,434 --> 00:31:10,712
Rod Atkins and John Sampson

520
00:31:10,736 --> 00:31:12,080
were there together,

521
00:31:12,104 --> 00:31:15,783
and they have a history of
hatred for the Vietnamese.

522
00:31:15,807 --> 00:31:19,054
They both spent six months
in the stockade at Long Binh

523
00:31:19,078 --> 00:31:21,222
for terrorizing
Vietnamese civilians.

524
00:31:21,246 --> 00:31:23,658
No kidding.

525
00:31:23,682 --> 00:31:26,794
And Deputy Atkins was off
duty that night, wasn't he?

526
00:31:26,818 --> 00:31:27,917
Yeah.

527
00:31:30,221 --> 00:31:31,588
He's our man.

528
00:31:39,331 --> 00:31:41,075
We have to prove...

529
00:31:41,099 --> 00:31:44,512
that he stole Mr. Tran's knife.

530
00:31:44,536 --> 00:31:46,414
Find Tyler.

531
00:31:46,438 --> 00:31:48,649
Go to Jackson's Fishing Supply,

532
00:31:48,673 --> 00:31:51,541
get a knife just
like Mr. Tran's.

533
00:31:52,978 --> 00:31:54,422
And let's just see
about something.

534
00:31:54,446 --> 00:31:55,924
<i>A continuance?</i>

535
00:31:55,948 --> 00:31:57,525
On what grounds, Mr. Matlock?

536
00:31:57,549 --> 00:31:59,060
Well, Your Honor,
some new information

537
00:31:59,084 --> 00:32:00,728
has come to my attention.

538
00:32:00,752 --> 00:32:02,664
I assure the court it's crucial.

539
00:32:02,688 --> 00:32:05,933
Defense has had ample
time to prepare its case.

540
00:32:05,957 --> 00:32:08,703
Your Honor, my entire defense

541
00:32:08,727 --> 00:32:09,904
rests on this information.

542
00:32:09,928 --> 00:32:11,973
All I need is 36 hours.

543
00:32:11,997 --> 00:32:15,476
All right. Court will
reconvene at 9 a.m. Thursday.

544
00:32:15,500 --> 00:32:16,700
Thank you.

545
00:32:19,304 --> 00:32:22,438
It's time, Mr. Jakes.

546
00:32:26,678 --> 00:32:27,744
You got a diver?

547
00:32:29,114 --> 00:32:30,524
Yep.

548
00:32:33,151 --> 00:32:34,529
You know where to look?

549
00:32:34,553 --> 00:32:35,864
Yeah.

550
00:32:35,888 --> 00:32:37,865
You're clear on how
to use this thing?

551
00:32:37,889 --> 00:32:39,922
Clear. And I'm cold.

552
00:32:52,104 --> 00:32:53,147
I got it!

553
00:32:53,171 --> 00:32:54,604
Bring it on in.

554
00:33:15,894 --> 00:33:19,640
Now we know Mr. Tran's knife
was not the murder weapon.

555
00:33:19,664 --> 00:33:21,175
That's good.

556
00:33:21,199 --> 00:33:22,376
Now we know.

557
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:24,212
Hey, will you guys
let me up on the boat?

558
00:33:24,236 --> 00:33:25,701
I'm freezing.

559
00:33:30,175 --> 00:33:31,685
Tyler.

560
00:33:31,709 --> 00:33:33,988
I'm sorry to do this to you.

561
00:33:34,012 --> 00:33:36,391
Maybe your body will adjust.

562
00:33:36,415 --> 00:33:40,283
You've got to try to find
the real murder weapon.

563
00:33:41,520 --> 00:33:42,985
This is bonus time again.

564
00:33:56,502 --> 00:33:58,468
They make a good sandwich?

565
00:34:00,405 --> 00:34:01,504
Fair.

566
00:34:05,143 --> 00:34:07,310
You lost your boat to the bank.

567
00:34:10,115 --> 00:34:11,559
I say, I heard you
were a fisherman,

568
00:34:11,583 --> 00:34:13,303
but you lost your
boat to the bank.

569
00:34:14,086 --> 00:34:15,296
That's right.

570
00:34:15,320 --> 00:34:17,087
Why don't you buy
John Sampson's boat?

571
00:34:18,723 --> 00:34:20,423
What do you want?

572
00:34:21,626 --> 00:34:23,326
Well... um...

573
00:34:25,931 --> 00:34:28,476
when John Sampson was killed,

574
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:29,577
where were you?

575
00:34:29,601 --> 00:34:31,734
I was home.

576
00:34:35,006 --> 00:34:37,785
I think you know a lot more

577
00:34:37,809 --> 00:34:39,454
about what happened

578
00:34:39,478 --> 00:34:43,045
to Mr. Sampson
than you're saying.

579
00:34:44,616 --> 00:34:46,583
Maybe even exactly.

580
00:34:47,786 --> 00:34:48,786
There you go.

581
00:34:51,122 --> 00:34:53,100
I'll see you in court.

582
00:35:08,807 --> 00:35:09,817
All right, Tom.

583
00:35:09,841 --> 00:35:11,761
Fine, I'll see you
in the morning.

584
00:35:13,912 --> 00:35:16,657
Did you know Tom Jakes
lost his son in Vietnam?

585
00:35:16,681 --> 00:35:18,025
No.

586
00:35:18,049 --> 00:35:19,093
Did he tell you that?

587
00:35:19,117 --> 00:35:20,861
No, I heard it in town.

588
00:35:20,885 --> 00:35:22,997
It was in 1972.

589
00:35:23,021 --> 00:35:25,366
Do you suppose that's why he
sponsored Mr. Tran and Lee?

590
00:35:25,390 --> 00:35:28,269
When was Tom's son killed?

591
00:35:28,293 --> 00:35:31,839
1972.

592
00:35:31,863 --> 00:35:34,609
That's the same
year Lee was born.

593
00:35:41,673 --> 00:35:43,384
Deputy Atkins, this...

594
00:35:43,408 --> 00:35:47,421
is a navigational
chart of Barton's Bay.

595
00:35:47,445 --> 00:35:50,758
And this... is where
Mr. Sampson's boat

596
00:35:50,782 --> 00:35:52,960
was at the time of the murder.

597
00:35:52,984 --> 00:35:55,696
Now, you say you
found Mr. Tran's knife

598
00:35:55,720 --> 00:35:58,232
directly under the boat,

599
00:35:58,256 --> 00:35:59,734
is that right? Yes.

600
00:35:59,758 --> 00:36:00,990
Actually...

601
00:36:03,328 --> 00:36:06,863
that's where you
planted it, isn't it?

602
00:36:07,966 --> 00:36:09,010
What are you talking about?

603
00:36:09,034 --> 00:36:11,245
I think you know.

604
00:36:11,269 --> 00:36:12,746
A few nights ago...

605
00:36:12,770 --> 00:36:15,216
there was a little experiment.

606
00:36:15,240 --> 00:36:17,352
Supervised by Mr. Tom Jakes,

607
00:36:17,376 --> 00:36:19,936
the harbormaster
of Barton's Bay.

608
00:36:22,647 --> 00:36:24,525
We purchased this knife.

609
00:36:24,549 --> 00:36:26,393
As you can see...

610
00:36:26,417 --> 00:36:30,464
it's, uh, an exact replica
of Mr. Tran's knife.

611
00:36:30,488 --> 00:36:32,367
We wanted to duplicate

612
00:36:32,391 --> 00:36:33,601
what would have happened

613
00:36:33,625 --> 00:36:35,502
to Mr. Tran's knife

614
00:36:35,526 --> 00:36:37,905
if it had been the
murder weapon.

615
00:36:37,929 --> 00:36:40,942
This knife was
dropped into the water

616
00:36:40,966 --> 00:36:45,012
from the stern of Mr. Sampson's
boat at exactly 1 a.m.,

617
00:36:45,036 --> 00:36:46,847
the time of the murder.

618
00:36:46,871 --> 00:36:48,282
The next morning,

619
00:36:48,306 --> 00:36:50,651
at approximately the same time

620
00:36:50,675 --> 00:36:53,588
you dived for Mr. Tran's knife,

621
00:36:53,612 --> 00:36:57,592
Mr. Tyler Hudson
dove for this one.

622
00:36:57,616 --> 00:36:59,983
And you know what?

623
00:37:01,419 --> 00:37:02,986
It wasn't there.

624
00:37:04,288 --> 00:37:05,466
You know where he found it?

625
00:37:05,490 --> 00:37:06,822
I don't know.

626
00:37:09,026 --> 00:37:11,494
It was found... here.

627
00:37:13,465 --> 00:37:18,601
More than 40 yards
south of the boat.

628
00:37:19,904 --> 00:37:21,471
How do you suppose it got there?

629
00:37:23,174 --> 00:37:24,719
I'll tell you.

630
00:37:24,743 --> 00:37:26,954
Tides.

631
00:37:26,978 --> 00:37:28,155
In less than nine hours,

632
00:37:28,179 --> 00:37:32,293
tides and heavy
currents carried it there.

633
00:37:32,317 --> 00:37:34,417
You know what that means?

634
00:37:35,754 --> 00:37:36,797
No.

635
00:37:36,821 --> 00:37:39,334
It means that Sheriff
Washburn was right

636
00:37:39,358 --> 00:37:41,802
about how Mr. Sampson was killed

637
00:37:41,826 --> 00:37:43,437
and what happened
to the murder weapon.

638
00:37:43,461 --> 00:37:46,696
The only thing is he
had the wrong knife.

639
00:37:48,233 --> 00:37:52,068
Mr. Hudson continued
to dive... and here...

640
00:37:55,240 --> 00:38:00,855
on the harbor bottom,
150 yards to the south...

641
00:38:00,879 --> 00:38:02,657
he found this,

642
00:38:02,681 --> 00:38:06,093
which I believe is the
real murder weapon.

643
00:38:06,117 --> 00:38:07,683
You see these initials?

644
00:38:09,086 --> 00:38:10,698
"J.S."

645
00:38:10,722 --> 00:38:13,100
John Sampson.

646
00:38:13,124 --> 00:38:15,469
He was killed
with his own knife.

647
00:38:15,493 --> 00:38:16,526
Maybe you knew that.

648
00:38:17,596 --> 00:38:19,040
No, I didn't.

649
00:38:19,064 --> 00:38:21,008
I'll tell you what you did know.

650
00:38:21,032 --> 00:38:24,878
When the sheriff asked you
to dive for the murder weapon...

651
00:38:24,902 --> 00:38:27,782
you saw an opportunity
to frame Mr. Tran.

652
00:38:27,806 --> 00:38:31,819
You and John Sampson
had stolen his knife,

653
00:38:31,843 --> 00:38:33,954
along with several other items,

654
00:38:33,978 --> 00:38:36,145
from his boat
several days before.

655
00:38:37,381 --> 00:38:38,525
With nobody noticing,

656
00:38:38,549 --> 00:38:40,561
you went into the water
with Mr. Tran's knife,

657
00:38:40,585 --> 00:38:42,363
swam around a few
minutes, and brought it up,

658
00:38:42,387 --> 00:38:43,786
didn't you?

659
00:38:46,291 --> 00:38:49,103
Deputy Atkins, you've
been having an affair...

660
00:38:49,127 --> 00:38:51,005
with John Sampson's wife.

661
00:38:51,029 --> 00:38:54,808
You were a fisherman
who lost his boat.

662
00:38:54,832 --> 00:38:57,912
I think Mr. Barton
could make a case.

663
00:38:57,936 --> 00:39:00,614
You wanted his
wife and his boat,

664
00:39:00,638 --> 00:39:02,550
and saw a chance
to get both of them.

665
00:39:02,574 --> 00:39:03,784
So you killed him.

666
00:39:03,808 --> 00:39:05,274
No. I didn't.

667
00:39:06,845 --> 00:39:08,311
No, you have to believe me.

668
00:39:10,449 --> 00:39:13,761
We robbed Tran and
we burned his boat...

669
00:39:13,785 --> 00:39:14,896
but I planted the knife

670
00:39:14,920 --> 00:39:17,040
because I thought
he killed John.

671
00:39:17,889 --> 00:39:20,835
And because you have a
hatred for the Vietnamese.

672
00:39:20,859 --> 00:39:21,835
Yes.

673
00:39:21,859 --> 00:39:23,259
I hate them.

674
00:39:24,896 --> 00:39:26,228
I didn't kill John.

675
00:39:29,600 --> 00:39:32,613
Your Honor...

676
00:39:32,637 --> 00:39:35,916
Deputy Atkins has
admitted to many things:

677
00:39:35,940 --> 00:39:40,120
robbery, arson,
false testimony...

678
00:39:40,144 --> 00:39:42,022
certainly bigotry.

679
00:39:42,046 --> 00:39:43,524
He has not admitted to murder.

680
00:39:43,548 --> 00:39:46,794
And I'm grateful...
that I don't bear

681
00:39:46,818 --> 00:39:48,818
the responsibility
of that proof.

682
00:39:52,056 --> 00:39:56,370
Your Honor, the prosecution's
case against my client

683
00:39:56,394 --> 00:39:59,740
hinged on Mr. Tran's knife...

684
00:39:59,764 --> 00:40:03,244
which we know now was
planted by Deputy Atkins.

685
00:40:03,268 --> 00:40:05,779
Under the circumstances...

686
00:40:05,803 --> 00:40:08,215
the defense moves for dismissal.

687
00:40:08,239 --> 00:40:09,517
Mr. Barton?

688
00:40:09,541 --> 00:40:11,352
No objection.

689
00:40:11,376 --> 00:40:12,720
This case is dismissed.

690
00:40:13,745 --> 00:40:14,889
Father.

691
00:40:22,421 --> 00:40:23,498
Thank you, Mr. Matlock.

692
00:40:23,522 --> 00:40:26,082
Thank you. LEE: Thank you.

693
00:40:52,717 --> 00:40:55,463
Well... good morning.

694
00:40:55,487 --> 00:40:57,564
Good morning.

695
00:40:57,588 --> 00:41:00,034
Well, I see they're
putting it back together.

696
00:41:00,058 --> 00:41:01,569
Gonna be a lot of work.

697
00:41:01,593 --> 00:41:03,203
Yeah, but they'll do it.

698
00:41:03,227 --> 00:41:04,338
Mr. Matlock...

699
00:41:04,362 --> 00:41:07,474
I didn't get a chance
to congratulate you.

700
00:41:07,498 --> 00:41:08,709
Well, thank you.

701
00:41:08,733 --> 00:41:10,811
You put on a good show.

702
00:41:10,835 --> 00:41:14,081
Well, sometimes I think
maybe a little too far.

703
00:41:17,074 --> 00:41:19,575
But, uh, I'll tell
you the truth. I...

704
00:41:21,579 --> 00:41:23,724
I never could have
done it without your help.

705
00:41:23,748 --> 00:41:24,825
How's that?

706
00:41:24,849 --> 00:41:27,762
Well... you're the one

707
00:41:27,786 --> 00:41:30,831
who recommended
that Tom Jakes hire me.

708
00:41:30,855 --> 00:41:32,432
I was just helping out a friend.

709
00:41:32,456 --> 00:41:33,656
Yeah.

710
00:41:35,226 --> 00:41:38,105
I didn't think that my client...

711
00:41:38,129 --> 00:41:40,774
could get a fair
trial at Barton's Bay.

712
00:41:40,798 --> 00:41:43,677
You argued against
change of venue, and then...

713
00:41:43,701 --> 00:41:46,013
Well, I would have done
the same thing by myself.

714
00:41:46,037 --> 00:41:49,049
Then... when...

715
00:41:49,073 --> 00:41:51,018
When somebody burned my new car,

716
00:41:51,042 --> 00:41:52,920
the judge reconsidered.

717
00:41:52,944 --> 00:41:54,121
And rightfully.

718
00:41:54,145 --> 00:41:56,490
Yeah. That was a
change of venue.

719
00:41:58,016 --> 00:42:02,063
I think, uh... you
burned my car.

720
00:42:02,087 --> 00:42:03,998
What?

721
00:42:04,022 --> 00:42:07,268
And when it looked like
I couldn't possibly win,

722
00:42:07,292 --> 00:42:08,769
you came to the rescue.

723
00:42:08,793 --> 00:42:11,861
It was very subtle. But you did.

724
00:42:12,998 --> 00:42:16,443
You told me all about the tides.

725
00:42:16,467 --> 00:42:17,978
What is all this?

726
00:42:18,002 --> 00:42:19,980
It's the most incredible thing

727
00:42:20,004 --> 00:42:21,737
I've ever been a part of.

728
00:42:24,642 --> 00:42:27,354
You didn't want Mr. Tran...

729
00:42:27,378 --> 00:42:30,791
to be found guilty of a
murder you had committed,

730
00:42:30,815 --> 00:42:32,360
so you...

731
00:42:32,384 --> 00:42:35,729
protected him the
best way you know how.

732
00:42:35,753 --> 00:42:39,066
In a court of law... through me.

733
00:42:39,090 --> 00:42:40,789
You're accusing me of murder?

734
00:42:43,094 --> 00:42:45,494
Look at this transcript.

735
00:42:51,068 --> 00:42:53,214
When you had Tom
Jakes on the stand,

736
00:42:53,238 --> 00:42:57,318
you asked him...
And I'm quoting you

737
00:42:57,342 --> 00:43:00,654
"Isn't it also true that
you sponsored Mr. Tran

738
00:43:00,678 --> 00:43:04,580
and his stepson when they
first came to Barton's Bay?"

739
00:43:05,783 --> 00:43:06,983
You called him stepson.

740
00:43:09,087 --> 00:43:11,647
Maybe you didn't
mean to do that.

741
00:43:11,923 --> 00:43:13,767
How did you know...

742
00:43:13,791 --> 00:43:16,937
Lee is not Mr. Tran's blood son?

743
00:43:16,961 --> 00:43:18,772
I don't know.

744
00:43:18,796 --> 00:43:21,609
Until he told me...

745
00:43:21,633 --> 00:43:23,910
there were only two other people

746
00:43:23,934 --> 00:43:26,213
besides Mr. Tran who knew:

747
00:43:26,237 --> 00:43:27,336
Tom Jakes...

748
00:43:29,874 --> 00:43:31,819
and Lee's real father.

749
00:43:31,843 --> 00:43:33,721
An American soldier who served

750
00:43:33,745 --> 00:43:36,256
in Vietnam in 1972.

751
00:43:36,280 --> 00:43:37,747
That's you, Mr. Barton.

752
00:43:38,849 --> 00:43:42,518
Mr. Matlock... I
loved his mother.

753
00:43:45,256 --> 00:43:46,522
Please, believe me.

754
00:43:49,127 --> 00:43:50,492
I believe you did.

755
00:43:52,030 --> 00:43:56,310
I believe you're a good
and honorable man.

756
00:43:56,334 --> 00:43:57,644
That's why I'm here.

757
00:43:57,668 --> 00:44:00,981
From the fall of Saigon in 1975,

758
00:44:01,005 --> 00:44:03,050
you made inquires...

759
00:44:03,074 --> 00:44:07,287
about Mr. Tran and his stepson.

760
00:44:07,311 --> 00:44:13,193
And just... two months...
before Tom Jakes

761
00:44:13,217 --> 00:44:15,662
asked you for legal help

762
00:44:15,686 --> 00:44:18,399
in sponsoring a
Vietnamese family,

763
00:44:18,423 --> 00:44:20,934
you found Mr. Tran and Lee

764
00:44:20,958 --> 00:44:22,998
in a refugee camp in Thailand.

765
00:44:25,463 --> 00:44:29,009
You selected
them... for Mr. Jakes.

766
00:44:31,235 --> 00:44:32,802
Seven years I looked for Lee.

767
00:44:34,338 --> 00:44:36,183
I was already
married with one child

768
00:44:36,207 --> 00:44:38,307
when I was in Vietnam
and met his mother.

769
00:44:40,011 --> 00:44:41,321
There was nothing
else I could do.

770
00:44:41,345 --> 00:44:42,523
I had to leave her.

771
00:44:42,547 --> 00:44:46,860
She said that... she
would say I was dead

772
00:44:46,884 --> 00:44:49,218
and marry Mr. Tran.

773
00:44:54,091 --> 00:44:56,937
I never forgot my son.

774
00:44:56,961 --> 00:44:58,060
I couldn't.

775
00:45:02,534 --> 00:45:05,034
Why did you kill John Sampson?

776
00:45:06,737 --> 00:45:08,282
Why?

777
00:45:08,306 --> 00:45:10,266
I didn't go there to kill him.

778
00:45:12,143 --> 00:45:14,688
I knew that he'd
burned the boat.

779
00:45:14,712 --> 00:45:16,712
He could have
caused Lee's death.

780
00:45:19,450 --> 00:45:22,730
I went there to tell him to
leave the Vietnamese alone.

781
00:45:22,754 --> 00:45:24,987
He laughed at me and
ordered me off his boat.

782
00:45:27,091 --> 00:45:28,435
I was very angry.

783
00:45:28,459 --> 00:45:31,071
He turned his
back on me, and I...

784
00:45:31,095 --> 00:45:32,839
saw his knife lying there,

785
00:45:32,863 --> 00:45:35,664
and I picked it up...
and I stabbed him.

786
00:45:38,002 --> 00:45:39,246
He pulled the knife from himself

787
00:45:39,270 --> 00:45:40,781
and he came for me.

788
00:45:40,805 --> 00:45:42,749
He fell by the back of the boat,

789
00:45:42,773 --> 00:45:44,507
dropped the knife
into the water.

790
00:45:45,577 --> 00:45:47,555
Heh.

791
00:45:47,579 --> 00:45:50,357
See, when the deputy
came up with Mr. Tran's knife,

792
00:45:50,381 --> 00:45:52,615
I knew that he was framed.

793
00:45:58,623 --> 00:45:59,722
You were right.

794
00:46:02,059 --> 00:46:04,159
I helped you with
your case, didn't I?

795
00:46:05,497 --> 00:46:07,140
Yeah.

796
00:46:07,164 --> 00:46:09,404
I couldn't have
done it without you.

797
00:46:19,510 --> 00:46:23,913
Well... I guess, uh... Yes.

